{:edb/db-before-t 6135 :edb/db-after-t 6136 :edb/tx-data [[13194139539448 50 #inst "2026-10-24T01:55:52.449Z" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186593303 1071 "If\n    $N=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 2 & -15\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${N}^{T}=$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186593303 1071 "If\n$N=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 2 & -15\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${N}^{T}=$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186597612 1071 "In a nature park, there are $4$ species of squirrels, species $1$, species $2$, species $3,$ and species $4.$ Each species has the same population.\n\n- $8%$ of squirrels of species $1$ are born in captivity.\n\n- $9%$ of squirrels of species $2$ are born in captivity.\n\n- $11%$ of squirrels of species $4$ are born in captivity.\n\nIf $42%$ of the squirrels are adult females, $8%$ of the adult female squirrels are born in captivity, and $36%$ of the squirrels born in captivity are adult females, what is the probability that a randomly chosen squirrel from species $3$ is born in captivity? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186597612 1071 "In a nature park, there are $4$ species of squirrels, species $1$, species $2$, species $3,$ and species $4.$ Each species has the same population.\n\n- $8\\%$ of squirrels of species $1$ are born in captivity.\n\n- $9\\%$ of squirrels of species $2$ are born in captivity.\n\n- $11\\%$ of squirrels of species $4$ are born in captivity.\n\nIf $42\\%$ of the squirrels are adult females, $8\\%$ of the adult female squirrels are born in captivity, and $36\\%$ of the squirrels born in captivity are adult females, what is the probability that a randomly chosen squirrel from species $3$ is born in captivity? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186597612 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a squirrel is an adult female, $C$ be the event that a squirrel is born in captivity, and ${S}_{1},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},{S}_{4}$ be the event that a squirrel is of species $1,$ $2,$ $3,$ or $4,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- Each species has the same number of individuals, so we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({S}_{1}) &=P({S}_{2})=P({S}_{3})=P({S}_{4})=0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(C|{S}_{1})=0.08 \\\\  & P(C|{S}_{2})=0.09 \\\\  & P(C|{S}_{4})=0.11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}⊔{S}_{3}⊔{S}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})+P({S}_{3})+P({S}_{4})=0.25+0.25+0.25+0.25=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $42%$ of the squirrels are adult females, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.42.\n$$\n\n- Since $8%$ of adult females are born in captivity, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|F)=0.08.\n$$\n\n- Since $36%$ of squirrels born in captivity are adult females, we have\n\n$$\nP(F|C)=0.36.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen squirrel from species $3$ is born in captivity, which is represented as $P(C|{S}_{3}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|C) & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C)} \\\\ 0.36 & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C|{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(C|{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})+P(C|{S}_{3})P({S}_{3})+P(C|{S}_{4})P({S}_{4})} \\\\ 0.36 & =\\frac{(0.08)(0.42)}{(0.08)(0.25)+(0.09)(0.25)+P(C|{S}_{3})(0.25)+(0.11)(0.25)} \\\\ 0.36 & =\\frac{0.0336}{0.07+0.25P(C|{S}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(C|{S}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.36(0.07+0.25P(C|{S}_{3})) & =0.0336 \\\\ 0.07+0.25P(C|{S}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.0336}{0.36} \\\\ 0.07+0.25P(C|{S}_{3}) & =\\frac{7}{75} \\\\ 0.25P(C|{S}_{3}) & =\\frac{7}{75}-0.07 \\\\ P(C|{S}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.25}(\\frac{7}{75}-0.07) \\\\ P(C|{S}_{3}) & ≈0.093\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186597612 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a squirrel is an adult female, $C$ be the event that a squirrel is born in captivity, and ${S}_{1},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},{S}_{4}$ be the event that a squirrel is of species $1,$ $2,$ $3,$ or $4,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- Each species has the same number of individuals, so we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({S}_{1}) &=P({S}_{2})=P({S}_{3})=P({S}_{4})=0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(C|{S}_{1})=0.08 \\\\  & P(C|{S}_{2})=0.09 \\\\  & P(C|{S}_{4})=0.11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}⊔{S}_{3}⊔{S}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})+P({S}_{3})+P({S}_{4})=0.25+0.25+0.25+0.25=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $42\\%$ of the squirrels are adult females, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.42.\n$$\n\n- Since $8\\%$ of adult females are born in captivity, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|F)=0.08.\n$$\n\n- Since $36\\%$ of squirrels born in captivity are adult females, we have\n\n$$\nP(F|C)=0.36.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen squirrel from species $3$ is born in captivity, which is represented as $P(C|{S}_{3}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|C) & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C)} \\\\ 0.36 & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C|{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(C|{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})+P(C|{S}_{3})P({S}_{3})+P(C|{S}_{4})P({S}_{4})} \\\\ 0.36 & =\\frac{(0.08)(0.42)}{(0.08)(0.25)+(0.09)(0.25)+P(C|{S}_{3})(0.25)+(0.11)(0.25)} \\\\ 0.36 & =\\frac{0.0336}{0.07+0.25P(C|{S}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(C|{S}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.36(0.07+0.25P(C|{S}_{3})) & =0.0336 \\\\ 0.07+0.25P(C|{S}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.0336}{0.36} \\\\ 0.07+0.25P(C|{S}_{3}) & =\\frac{7}{75} \\\\ 0.25P(C|{S}_{3}) & =\\frac{7}{75}-0.07 \\\\ P(C|{S}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.25}(\\frac{7}{75}-0.07) \\\\ P(C|{S}_{3}) & ≈0.093\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186601514 1074 "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nSince\n $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$ and\n    $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\n$$\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{2}+\\text{i}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =4+4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186601514 1074 "To express the number in Cartesian form, we expand the parentheses and substitute the values of the cosine and sine.\n\nSince\n $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$ and\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\n$$\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{2}+\\text{i}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =4+4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186601943 1074 "Let $A$ denote the event described in the question. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{n}{N},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the total number of ways to draw $3$ balls out of $20,$ and $n$ is the number of ways to draw exactly $2$ black and $1$ white balls.\n\nNote that since the order of the draws does not matter, we use combinations.\n\nFirst, we compute $N.$ The number of ways to draw $3$ balls out of $20$  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N={}_{20}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{20!}{3!\\cdot 17!}=1\\,140.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $n.$ The computation of $n$ can be divided into two steps: \n\n- the number of ways to draw $1$ (out of $10$) white ball is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{10}{C}_{1} &=\\frac{10!}{1!\\cdot 9!}=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the number of ways to draw $2$ (out of $10$) black balls is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{10}{C}_{2} & =\\frac{10!}{2!\\cdot 8!}=45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the results from the above steps, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n &={}_{10}{C}_{1}\\cdot {}_{10}{C}_{2}=10\\cdot 45=450.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{450}{1\\,140}≈0.39=39%.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186601943 1074 "Let $A$ denote the event described in the question. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{n}{N},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the total number of ways to draw $3$ balls out of $20,$ and $n$ is the number of ways to draw exactly $2$ black and $1$ white balls.\n\nNote that since the order of the draws does not matter, we use combinations.\n\nFirst, we compute $N.$ The number of ways to draw $3$ balls out of $20$  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N={}_{20}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{20!}{3!\\cdot 17!}=1\\,140.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $n.$ The computation of $n$ can be divided into two steps: \n\n- the number of ways to draw $1$ (out of $10$) white ball is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{10}{C}_{1} &=\\frac{10!}{1!\\cdot 9!}=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the number of ways to draw $2$ (out of $10$) black balls is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{10}{C}_{2} & =\\frac{10!}{2!\\cdot 8!}=45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the results from the above steps, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n &={}_{10}{C}_{1}\\cdot {}_{10}{C}_{2}=10\\cdot 45=450.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{450}{1\\,140}≈0.39=39\\%.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186604566 1071 "Suppose that $32%$ of the population of office workers in a particular city plan to go to the beach next summer. A random sample of $n=45$ office workers is taken from the population and asked about their vacation plans.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ the proportion of office workers in the sample who plan to go to a beach next summer? Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186604566 1071 "Suppose that $32\\%$ of the population of office workers in a particular city plan to go to the beach next summer. A random sample of $n=45$ office workers is taken from the population and asked about their vacation plans.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ the proportion of office workers in the sample who plan to go to a beach next summer? Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186604566 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=32%=0.32,$ and $n=45.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}] & =32%=0.32, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.32(1-0.32)}{45} \\\\  & ≈0.0048\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186604566 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=32\\%=0.32,$ and $n=45.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}] & =32\\%=0.32, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.32(1-0.32)}{45} \\\\  & ≈0.0048\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186604573 1071 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=90$ from a population where $10%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,90.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{90}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186604573 1071 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=90$ from a population where $10\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,90.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{90}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186604573 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p),$ where\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=10%=0.1,\\,n=90.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(90,0.1)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =90\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =9 \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =90\\cdot 0.1\\cdot (1-0.1) \\\\  & =8.1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186604573 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p),$ where\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=10\\%=0.1,\\,n=90.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(90,0.1)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =90\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =9 \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =90\\cdot 0.1\\cdot (1-0.1) \\\\  & =8.1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186604582 1071 "There are $30$ students in the $6$th grade of Arizona Elementary School. From this population of students, $20%$ have blue eyes. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population has blue eyes} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,30.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=1).$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186604582 1071 "There are $30$ students in the $6$th grade of Arizona Elementary School. From this population of students, $20\\%$ have blue eyes. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population has blue eyes} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,30.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=1).$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186604604 1071 "Suppose we have a population of size $N=230,$ where $40%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be eq