ded tiles out of $100.$\n\nTherefore, $54\\%$ of the square is shaded." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186606912 1074 "One large square representing one whole is divided into $100$ equal tiles.\n\nWe have $1$ fully shaded large square ($100$ shaded tiles). Additionally, we have $25$ shaded tiles in the second large square. Hence, there are $125$ shaded tiles in total.\n\nTherefore, the model represents $125%.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186606912 1074 "One large square representing one whole is divided into $100$ equal tiles.\n\nWe have $1$ fully shaded large square ($100$ shaded tiles). Additionally, we have $25$ shaded tiles in the second large square. Hence, there are $125$ shaded tiles in total.\n\nTherefore, the model represents $125\\%.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186609817 1071 "A bakery sold $200$ loaves of bread last week. This week, sales are $400%$ greater than last week. How many loaves of bread did the bakery sell this week?\n\nThe bakery sold\n{{field-1}}\nloaves of bread this week." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186609817 1071 "A bakery sold $200$ loaves of bread last week. This week, sales are $400\\%$ greater than last week. How many loaves of bread did the bakery sell this week?\n\nThe bakery sold\n{{field-1}}\nloaves of bread this week." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186609817 1074 "To find this week's bread sales, we apply a $400%$ increase to last week's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $400%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+400%=500%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n500%=\\frac{500}{100}=5\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original amount by $5:$\n\n$$\n200\\times 5=1,000\n$$\n\nSo, the bakery sold $1000$ loaves of bread this week." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186609817 1074 "To find this week's bread sales, we apply a $400\\%$ increase to last week's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $400\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+400\\%=500\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n500\\%=\\frac{500}{100}=5\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original amount by $5:$\n\n$$\n200\\times 5=1,000\n$$\n\nSo, the bakery sold $1000$ loaves of bread this week." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186609823 1071 "What number is $300%$ greater than $18?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186609823 1071 "What number is $300\\%$ greater than $18?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186609823 1074 "To find a number $300%$ *greater than* $18,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+300%=400%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n400%=\\frac{400}{100}=4\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $300%$ greater than $18,$ we multiply $18$ by $4:$\n\n$$\n4\\cdot 18=72\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $300%$ greater than $18$ is $72.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186609823 1074 "To find a number $300\\%$ *greater than* $18,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+300\\%=400\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n400\\%=\\frac{400}{100}=4\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $300\\%$ greater than $18,$ we multiply $18$ by $4:$\n\n$$\n4\\cdot 18=72\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $300\\%$ greater than $18$ is $72.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186609827 1071 "What number is $40%$ greater than $350?$\n\nThe increased number is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186609827 1071 "What number is $40\\%$ greater than $350?$\n\nThe increased number is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186609827 1074 "To find a number $40%$ *greater than* $350,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+40%=140%.\n$$\n\nWe convert this percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n140%=\\frac{140}{100}=1.4\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original number by $1.4:$\n\n$$\n350\\times 1.4=490\n$$\n\nSo, the number that is $40%$ greater than $350$ is $490.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186609827 1074 "To find a number $40\\%$ *greater than* $350,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+40\\%=140\\%.\n$$\n\nWe convert this percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n140\\%=\\frac{140}{100}=1.4\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original number by $1.4:$\n\n$$\n350\\times 1.4=490\n$$\n\nSo, the number that is $40\\%$ greater than $350$ is $490.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186609828 1071 "Find $40%$ of $60,$ and then $25%$ of the result." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186609828 1071 "Find $40\\%$ of $60,$ and then $25\\%$ of the result." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186609828 1074 "First, we find $40%$ of $60:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40%\\times 60 & =\\frac{40}{100}\\times 60 \\\\  & =0.4\\times 60 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $25%$ of the result $(24):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25%\\times 24 & =\\frac{25}{100}\\times 24 \\\\  & =0.25\\times 24 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final answer is $6.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186609828 1074 "First, we find $40\\%$ of $60:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40\\%\\times 60 & =\\frac{40}{100}\\times 60 \\\\  & =0.4\\times 60 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $25\\%$ of the result $(24):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25\\%\\times 24 & =\\frac{25}{100}\\times 24 \\\\  & =0.25\\times 24 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final answer is $6.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186609841 1071 "A city’s population rose to $160%$ of its previous size due to natural growth. Then, migration added $15%$ of that new population. If the city originally had $50,000$ people, how many people were added by migration?\n\nThe number of people added by migration is  \n{{field-1}}." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186609841 1071 "A city’s population rose to $160\\%$ of its previous size due to natural growth. Then, migration added $15\\%$ of that new population. If the city originally had $50,000$ people, how many people were added by migration?\n\nThe number of people added by migration is  \n{{field-1}}." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186609841 1074 "First, the new population is $160%$ of the original $(50,000$ people$):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}160%\\times 50,000 & =\\frac{160}{100}\\times 50,000 \\\\  & =1.6\\times 50,000 \\\\  & =80,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the new population is $80,000$ people. Then, the people added by migration is $15%$ of this new population:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15%\\times 80,000 & =\\frac{15}{100}\\times 80,000 \\\\  & =0.15\\times 80,000 \\\\  & =12,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the people added by migration is $12,000.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186609841 1074 "First, the new population is $160\\%$ of the original $(50,000$ people$):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}160\\%\\times 50,000 & =\\frac{160}{100}\\times 50,000 \\\\  & =1.6\\times 50,000 \\\\  & =80,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the new population is $80,000$ people. Then, the people added by migration is $15\\%$ of this new population:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\%\\times 80,000 & =\\frac{15}{100}\\times 80,000 \\\\  & =0.15\\times 80,000 \\\\  & =12,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the people added by migration is $12,000.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186609843 1071 "What number is $45%$ greater than $220?$\n\nThe increased number is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186609843 1071 "What number is $45\\%$ greater than $220?$\n\nThe increased number is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186609843 1074 "To find a number $45%$ *greater than* $220,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+45%=145%.\n$$\n\nWe convert this percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n145%=\\frac{145}{100}=1.45\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original number by $1.45:$\n\n$$\n220\\times 1.45=319\n$$\n\nSo, the number that is $45%$ greater than $220$ is $319.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186609843 1074 "To find a number $45\\%$ *greater than* $220,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+45\\%=145\\%.\n$$\n\nWe convert this percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n145\\%=\\frac{145}{100}=1.45\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original number by $1.45:$\n\n$$\n220\\times 1.45=319\n$$\n\nSo, the number that is $45\\%$ greater than $220$ is $319.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186609849 1071 "A bakery sold $500$ loaves of bread last month. This month, sales are $200%$ greater than last month. How many loaves of bread did the bakery sell this month?\n\nThe bakery sold\n{{field-1}}\nloaves of bread this month." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186609849 1071 "A bakery sold $500$ loaves of bread last month. This month, sales are $200\\%$ greater than last month. How many loaves of bread did the bakery sell this month?\n\nThe bakery sold\n{{field-1}}\nloaves of bread this month." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186609849 1074 "To find this month's bread sales, we apply a $200%$ increase to last month's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $200%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+200%=300%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n300%=\\frac{300}{100}=3\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original amount by $3:$\n\n$$\n500\\times 3=1,500\n$$\n\nSo, the bakery sold $1500$ loaves of bread this month." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186609849 1074 "To find this month's bread sales, we apply a $200\\%$ increase to last month's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $200\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+200\\%=300\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n300\\%=\\frac{300}{100}=3\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original amount by $3:$\n\n$$\n500\\times 3=1,500\n$$\n\nSo, the bakery sold $1500$ loaves of bread this month." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186609850 1071 "A library’s book collection expanded to $150%$ of its previous size. Of t