ore, the total number of apartments in the building is $80.$ The number of available apartments is\n\n$$\n80-68=12.\n$$\n\nHence, $12$ apartments are still available." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186613219 1074 "We need to know the total number of apartments in the building to determine the number of available apartments. \n\nWe're given a part and a percentage, and we need to find the whole.\n\nIn this case, the part is the number of occupied apartments, which is $68.$ The percentage is $85\\%,$ and the whole is the total number of apartments. \n\nTo find the whole, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{whole}=\\text{part}÷\\text{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $85\\%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{85}{100}=\\frac{85÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{17}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =68÷85\\% \\\\  & =68÷\\frac{17}{20} \\\\  & =68\\times \\frac{20}{17} \\\\  & =\\frac{68}{17}\\times 20 \\\\  & =4\\times 20 \\\\  & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total number of apartments in the building is $80.$ The number of available apartments is\n\n$$\n80-68=12.\n$$\n\nHence, $12$ apartments are still available." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186616122 1071 "A warehouse shipped $150$ packages on Monday. On Tuesday, shipments are $180%$ greater than on Monday. How many packages did the warehouse ship on Tuesday?\n\nThe warehouse shipped\n{{field-1}}\npackages on Tuesday." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186616122 1071 "A warehouse shipped $150$ packages on Monday. On Tuesday, shipments are $180\\%$ greater than on Monday. How many packages did the warehouse ship on Tuesday?\n\nThe warehouse shipped\n{{field-1}}\npackages on Tuesday." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186616122 1074 "To find Tuesday's shipments, we apply a $180%$ increase to Monday's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $180%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+180%=280%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n280%=\\frac{280}{100}=2.8\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original amount by $2.8:$\n\n$$\n150\\times 2.8=420\n$$\n\nSo, the warehouse shipped $420$ packages on Tuesday." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186616122 1074 "To find Tuesday's shipments, we apply a $180\\%$ increase to Monday's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $180\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+180\\%=280\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n280\\%=\\frac{280}{100}=2.8\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original amount by $2.8:$\n\n$$\n150\\times 2.8=420\n$$\n\nSo, the warehouse shipped $420$ packages on Tuesday." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186616131 1071 "What number, when increased by $165%,$ becomes $212?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186616131 1071 "What number, when increased by $165\\%,$ becomes $212?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186616131 1074 "Increasing a number by $165%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+165%=265%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n265%=\\frac{265}{100}=2.65.\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the original number. Since the increased number equals $212,$ we have the equation\n\n$$\n2.65x=212.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{212}{2.65} \\\\  & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the original number is $80.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186616131 1074 "Increasing a number by $165\\%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+165\\%=265\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n265\\%=\\frac{265}{100}=2.65.\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the original number. Since the increased number equals $212,$ we have the equation\n\n$$\n2.65x=212.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{212}{2.65} \\\\  & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the original number is $80.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186616132 1071 "A charity’s weekend fundraiser brought in $\\$8,500$ on Saturday. On Sunday, donations were $18%$ greater than on Saturday. How many dollars were donated on Sunday?\n\nDonations on Sunday were\n{{field-1}}\ndollars." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186616132 1071 "A charity’s weekend fundraiser brought in $\\$8,500$ on Saturday. On Sunday, donations were $18\\%$ greater than on Saturday. How many dollars were donated on Sunday?\n\nDonations on Sunday were\n{{field-1}}\ndollars." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186616132 1074 "To find Sunday's donation total,  we apply an $18%$ increase to Saturday's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $18%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+18%=118%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n118%=\\frac{118}{100}=1.18\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original donations by $1.18:$\n\n$$\n8,500\\times 1.18=10,030\n$$\n\nSo, donations on Sunday were $10,030$ dollars." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186616132 1074 "To find Sunday's donation total,  we apply an $18\\%$ increase to Saturday's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $18\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+18\\%=118\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n118\\%=\\frac{118}{100}=1.18\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original donations by $1.18:$\n\n$$\n8,500\\times 1.18=10,030\n$$\n\nSo, donations on Sunday were $10,030$ dollars." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186616137 1071 "What number, when increased by $250%,$ becomes $525?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186616137 1071 "What number, when increased by $250\\%,$ becomes $525?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186616137 1074 "Increasing a number by $250%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+250%=350%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n350%=\\frac{350}{100}=3.5.\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the original number. Since the increased number equals $525,$ we have the equation\n\n$$\n3.5x=525.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{525}{3.5} \\\\  & =150\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the original number is $150.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186616137 1074 "Increasing a number by $250\\%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+250\\%=350\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n350\\%=\\frac{350}{100}=3.5.\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the original number. Since the increased number equals $525,$ we have the equation\n\n$$\n3.5x=525.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{525}{3.5} \\\\  & =150\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the original number is $150.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186616145 1071 "After increasing a quantity $x$ by $55%,$ the result is $62.$ What is the value of $x$?\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186616145 1071 "After increasing a quantity $x$ by $55\\%,$ the result is $62.$ What is the value of $x$?\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186616145 1074 "Increasing a number by $55%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+55%=155%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n155%=\\frac{155}{100}=1.55.\n$$\n\nWe're told that by increasing a number $x$ by $55%,$ we get the number $62.$ So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n1.55x=62.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{62}{1.55} \\\\  & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $x=40.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186616145 1074 "Increasing a number by $55\\%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+55\\%=155\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n155\\%=\\frac{155}{100}=1.55.\n$$\n\nWe're told that by increasing a number $x$ by $55\\%,$ we get the number $62.$ So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n1.55x=62.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{62}{1.55} \\\\  & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $x=40.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186616148 1071 "During a holiday week, a museum had $1,200$ visitors. The following holiday week, visitors were $25%$ greater. How many visitors did the museum have during the following holiday week?\n\nThe museum had\n{{field-1}}\nvisitors the following holiday week." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186616148 1071 "During a holiday week, a museum had $1,200$ visitors. The following holiday week, visitors were $25\\%$ greater. How many visitors did the museum have during the following holiday week?\n\nThe museum had\n{{field-1}}\nvisitors the following holiday week." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186616148 1074 "To find the number of visitors during the next holiday week, we apply a $25%$ increase to the previous holiday week's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $25%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+25%=125%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n125%=\\frac{125}{100}=1.25\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original number of visitors by $1.25:$\n\n$$\n1,200\\times 1.25=1,500\n$$\n\nSo, the museum had $1,500$ visitors the following holiday week." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186616148 1074 "To find the number of visitors during the next holiday week, we apply a $25\\%$ increase to the previous holiday week's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $25\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+25\\%=125\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n125\\%=\\frac{125}{100}=1.25\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original number of visitors by $1.25:$\n\n$$\n1,200\\times 1.25=1,500\n$$\n\nSo, the museum had $1,500$ visitors the following holiday week." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186616150 1071 "A number is increased by $260%$ to yield $90.$ What is the original number?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186616150 1071 "A number is increased by $260\\%$ to yield $90.$ What is the original number?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186616150 1074 "Increasing a number by $260%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+260%=360%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n360%=\\frac{360}{100}=3.60.\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the original number. Since the increased number equals $90,$ we have the equation\n\n$$\n3.60x=90.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{90}{3.60} \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the original number is $25.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186616150 1074 "Increasing a number by $260\\%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+260\\%=360\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n360\\%=\\frac{360}{100}=3.60.\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the original number. Since the increased number equals $90,$ we have the equation\n\n$$\n3.60x=90.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{90}{3.60} \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the original number is $25.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186616159 1071 "A number is increased by $150%$ to yield $75.$ What is the original number?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186616159 1071 "A number is increased by $150\\%$ to yield $75.$ What is the original number?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186616159 1074 "Increasing a number by $150%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+150%=250%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n250%=\\frac{250}{100}=2.5.\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the original number. Since the increased number equals $75,$ we have the equation\n\n$$\n2.5x=75.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{75}{2.5} \\\\  & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the original number is $30.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186616159 1074 "Increasing a number by $150\\%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+150\\%=250\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n250\\%=\\frac{250}{100}=2.5.\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the original number. Since the increased number equals $75,$ we have the equation\n\n$$\n2.5x=75.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{75}{2.5} \\\\  & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the original number is $30.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186616173 1071 "After a $20%$ increase, the price of a concert ticket is $\\$72.$ What was the original price?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186616173 1071 "After a $20\\%$ increase, the price of a concert ticket is $\\$72.$ What was the original price?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186616173 1074 "Increasing a number by $20%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+20%=120%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n120%=\\frac{120}{100}=1.20.\n$$\n\nLet the original price be $x.$ So, after a $20%$ increase, the new value is \n\n$$\n1.20x.\n$$\n\nWe are told the increased value is $\\$72.$ So, we set them equal and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.20x & =72 \\\\ x & =\\frac{72}{1.20} \\\\ x & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the original price was $\\$60.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186616173 1074 "Increasing a number by $20\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%+20\\%=120\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n120\\%=\\frac{120}{100}=1.20.\n$$\n\nLet the original price be $x.$ So, after a $20\\%$ increase, the new value is \n\n$$\n1.20x.\n$$\n\nWe are told the increased value is $\\$72.$ So, we set them equal and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.20x & =72 \\\\ x & =\\frac{72}{1.20} \\\\ x & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the original price was $\\$60.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186616182 1071 "A farm harvested $400$ pumpkins last season. This season, the harvest is $200%$ greater