d{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $x=60.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186625546 1071 "A student's savings account balance grew to $130%$ of its previous total. Of this new balance, $15%$ came from earned interest. What percentage of the previous total balance came from earned interest?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186625546 1071 "A student's savings account balance grew to $130\\%$ of its previous total. Of this new balance, $15\\%$ came from earned interest. What percentage of the previous total balance came from earned interest?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186625546 1074 "Let $x$ be the previous total balance.\n\nWe're told that the total balance grew to $130%$ of its previous total. Thus, the current balance is $1.30x.$\n\nWe are also told that of this new balance, $15%$ came from earned interest. So, the amount that came from earned interest is\n\n$$\n0.15(1.30x)=0.195x.\n$$\n\nThe expression $0.195x$ represents $19.5%$ of $x.$ Therefore, $19.5%$ of the previous total balance came from earned interest." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186625546 1074 "Let $x$ be the previous total balance.\n\nWe're told that the total balance grew to $130\\%$ of its previous total. Thus, the current balance is $1.30x.$\n\nWe are also told that of this new balance, $15\\%$ came from earned interest. So, the amount that came from earned interest is\n\n$$\n0.15(1.30x)=0.195x.\n$$\n\nThe expression $0.195x$ represents $19.5\\%$ of $x.$ Therefore, $19.5\\%$ of the previous total balance came from earned interest." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186625551 1071 "After decreasing a quantity $x$ by $10%,$ the result is $54.$ What is the value of $x?$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186625551 1071 "After decreasing a quantity $x$ by $10\\%,$ the result is $54.$ What is the value of $x?$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186625551 1074 "Decreasing a number by $10%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100%-10%=90%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n90%=\\frac{90}{100}=0.9.\n$$\n\nWe're told that by decreasing a number $x$ by $10%,$ we get the number $54.$ So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.9x=54.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{54}{0.9} \\\\  & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $x=60.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186625551 1074 "Decreasing a number by $10\\%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%-10\\%=90\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n90\\%=\\frac{90}{100}=0.9.\n$$\n\nWe're told that by decreasing a number $x$ by $10\\%,$ we get the number $54.$ So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.9x=54.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{54}{0.9} \\\\  & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $x=60.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186625558 1071 "After decreasing a quantity $x$ by $30%,$ the result is $49.$ What is the value of $x?$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186625558 1071 "After decreasing a quantity $x$ by $30\\%,$ the result is $49.$ What is the value of $x?$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186625558 1074 "Decreasing a number by $30%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100%-30%=70%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n70%=\\frac{70}{100}=0.7.\n$$\n\nWe're told that by decreasing a number $x$ by $30%,$ we get the number $49.$ So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.7x=49.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{49}{0.7} \\\\  & =70\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $x=70.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186625558 1074 "Decreasing a number by $30\\%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%-30\\%=70\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n70\\%=\\frac{70}{100}=0.7.\n$$\n\nWe're told that by decreasing a number $x$ by $30\\%,$ we get the number $49.$ So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.7x=49.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{49}{0.7} \\\\  & =70\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $x=70.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186625559 1071 "After a $20%$ population decrease, the number of residents in a town became $8,000.$ What was the original population?\n\nThe original population of the town was\n{{field-1}}." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186625559 1071 "After a $20\\%$ population decrease, the number of residents in a town became $8,000.$ What was the original population?\n\nThe original population of the town was\n{{field-1}}." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186625559 1074 "Decreasing a number by $20%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%-20%=80%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n80%=\\frac{80}{100}=0.8.\n$$\n\nLet the original population be $x.$ So, after a $20%$ decrease, the new value is\n\n$$\n0.8x.\n$$\n\nWe are told the new population is $8,000.$ So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.8x=8000.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.8x & =8000 \\\\ x & =\\frac{8000}{0.8} \\\\ x & =10000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the original population was $10,000.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186625559 1074 "Decreasing a number by $20\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%-20\\%=80\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n80\\%=\\frac{80}{100}=0.8.\n$$\n\nLet the original population be $x.$ So, after a $20\\%$ decrease, the new value is\n\n$$\n0.8x.\n$$\n\nWe are told the new population is $8,000.$ So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.8x=8000.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.8x & =8000 \\\\ x & =\\frac{8000}{0.8} \\\\ x & =10000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the original population was $10,000.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186625560 1071 "A soccer team has a $70%$ chance of winning each game in an $8$-game tournament. What is the probability that the team wins at least $7$ of the $8$ games? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186625560 1071 "A soccer team has a $70\\%$ chance of winning each game in an $8$-game tournament. What is the probability that the team wins at least $7$ of the $8$ games? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186625560 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of games that the team wins.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ games\n\n- the probability of winning each game is $p=70%=0.7$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(8,0.7).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.7)^{x}(1-0.7)^{8-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.7)^{x}(0.3)^{8-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the soccer team wins at least $7$ of the $8$ games. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 7) & =P(X=7)+P(X=8) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{7})(0.7)^{7}(0.3)^{8-7}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{8})(0.7)^{8}(0.3)^{8-8} \\\\  & =8(0.7)^{7}(0.3)+1(0.7)^{8} \\\\  & =0.255\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186625560 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of games that the team wins.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ games\n\n- the probability of winning each game is $p=70\\%=0.7$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(8,0.7).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.7)^{x}(1-0.7)^{8-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.7)^{x}(0.3)^{8-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the soccer team wins at least $7$ of the $8$ games. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 7) & =P(X=7)+P(X=8) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{7})(0.7)^{7}(0.3)^{8-7}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{8})(0.7)^{8}(0.3)^{8-8} \\\\  & =8(0.7)^{7}(0.3)+1(0.7)^{8} \\\\  & =0.255\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186625564 1071 "In the emergency room of a hospital, $45%$ of patients admitted are due to a traffic accident. If $8$ patients are admitted to the emergency room, what is the probability that at most $6$ of the admissions are due to traffic accidents? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186625564 1071 "In the emergency room of a hospital, $45\\%$ of patients admitted are due to a traffic accident. If $8$ patients are admitted to the emergency room, what is the probability that at most $6$ of the admissions are due to traffic accidents? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186625564 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of patients admitted due to traffic accidents.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ patients\n\n- the probability that each patient is admitted due to a traffic accident is $p=45%=0.45$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(8,0.45).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.45)^{x}(1-0.55)^{8-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.45)^{x}(0.55)^{8-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at most $6$ of the patients are due to traffic accidents. This corresponds to $P(X\\le 6).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 6)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=6)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =1-P(X>6) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=7)+P(X=8)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{7})(0.45)^{7}(0.55)^{8-7}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{8})(0.45)^{8}(0.55)^{8-8}] \\\\  & =1-[8(0.45)^{7}(0.55)+(0.45)^{8}] \\\\  & =1-0.018\\,123 \\\\  & =0.982\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186625564 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of patients admitted due to traffic accidents.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ patients\n\n- the probability that each patient is admitted due to a traffic accident is $p=45\\%=0.45$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(8,0.45).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.45)^{x}(1-0.55)^{8-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.45)^{x}(0.55)^{8-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at most $6$ of the patients are due to traffic accidents. This corresponds to $P(X\\le 6).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 6)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=6)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =1-P(X>6) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=7)+P(X=8)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{7})(0.45)^{7}(0.55)^{8-7}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{8})(0.45)^{8}(0.55)^{8-8}] \\\\  & =1-[8(0.45)^{7}(0.55)+(0.45)^{8}] \\\\  & =1-0.018\\,123 \\\\  & =0.982\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186625570 1071 "According to historical data, $85%$ of moviegoers said they liked watching movies. Of those, $20%$ preferred comedies. What percentage of all moviegoers preferred comedies?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186625570 1071 "According to historical data, $85\\%$ of moviegoers said they liked watching movies. Of those, $20\\%$ preferred comedies. What percentage of all moviegoers preferred comedies?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186625570 1074 "Let $x$ be the total number of moviegoers.\n\nWe're told that $85%$ of moviegoers liked watching movies. Thus, the number of moviegoers who like movies is $0.85x.$\n\nWe are also told that of those moviegoers, $20%$ preferred comedies. So, the number of moviegoers who preferred comedies is\n\n$$\n0.20(0.85x)=0.17x.\n$$\n\nThe expression $0.17x$ represents $17%$ of $x.$ Therefore, $17%$ of all moviegoers preferred comedies." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186625570 1074 "Let $x$ be the total number of moviegoers.\n\nWe're told that $85\\%$ of moviegoers liked watching movies. Thus, the number of moviegoers who like movies is $0.85x.$\n\nWe are also told that of those moviegoers, $20\\%$ preferred comedies. So, the number of moviegoers who preferred comedies is\n\n$$\n0.20(0.85x)=0.17x.\n$$\n\nThe expression $0.17x$ represents $17\\%$ of $x.$ Therefore, $17\\%$ of all moviegoers preferred comedies." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186625574 1071 "After decreasing a quantity $x$ by $15%,$ the result is $68.$ What is the value of $x?$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186625574 1071 "After decreasing a quantity $x$ by $15\\%,$ the result is $68.$ What is the value of $x?$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186625574 1074 "Decreasing a number by $15%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100%-15%=85%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n85%=\\frac{85}{100}=0.85.\n$$\n\nWe're told that by decreasing a number $x$ by $15%,$ we get the number $68.$ So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.85x=68.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{68}{0.85} \\\\  & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $x=80.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186625574 1074 "Decreasing a number by $15\\%$ means that we must apply a percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%-15\\%=85\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n85\\%=\\frac{85}{100}=0.85.\n$$\n\nWe're told that by decreasing a number $x$ by $15\\%,$ we get the number $68.$ So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.85x=68.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{68}{0.85} \\\\  & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $x=80.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186625575 1071 "At a fruit stand, some apples were sold. After $15%$ of the original apples were sold, $340$ apples remained. How many apples were sold?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186625575 1071 "At a fruit stand, some apples were sold. After $15\\%$ of the original apples were sold, $340$ apples remained. How many apples were sold?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186625575 1074 "Decreasing a number by $15%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%-15%=85%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n85%=\\frac{85}{100}=0.85.\n$$\n\nLet the original number of apples be $x.$ So, after a $15%$ decrease, the new value is \n\n$$\n0.85x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $340$ apples remained. So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.85x=340.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.85x & =340 \\\\ x & =\\frac{340}{0.85} \\\\ x & =400\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the original number of apples was $400,$ we find the number removed by taking $15%$ of $400:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{100}\\cdot 400=60.\n$$\n\nSo, $60$ apples were sold." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186625575 1074 "Decreasing a number by $15\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%-15\\%=85\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n85\\%=\\frac{85}{100}=0.85.\n$$\n\nLet the original number of apples be $x.$ So, after a $15\\%$ decrease, the new value is \n\n$$\n0.85x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $340$ apples remained. So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.85x=340.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.85x & =340 \\\\ x & =\\frac{340}{0.85} \\\\ x & =400\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the original number of apples was $400,$ we find the number removed by taking $15\\%$ of $400:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{100}\\cdot 400=60.\n$$\n\nSo, $60$ apples were sold." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186625577 1071 "After a $10%$ salary cut, an employee now earns $\\$900$ per week. What was the original salary?\n\nThe original weekly salary was $\\$${{field-1}}." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186625577 1071 "After a $10\\%$ salary cut, an employee now earns $\\$900$ per week. What was the original salary?\n\nThe original weekly salary was $\\$${{field-1}}." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186625577 1074 "Decreasing a number by $10%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%-10%=90%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, w