\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{18}{x})(0.15)^{x}(1-0.15)^{18-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{18}{x})(0.15)^{x}(0.85)^{18-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that there are at least $2$ spam emails. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=18)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{18}{0})(0.15)^{0}(0.85)^{18-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{18}{1})(0.15)^{1}(0.85)^{18-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.85)^{18}+16(0.15)(0.85)^{17}] \\\\  & =1-0.284\\,053 \\\\  & =0.776\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186625583 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of spam emails.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=18$ inbox emails\n\n- the probability of being a spam email is $p=15\\%=0.15$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(18,0.15).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{18}{x})(0.15)^{x}(1-0.15)^{18-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{18}{x})(0.15)^{x}(0.85)^{18-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that there are at least $2$ spam emails. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=18)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{18}{0})(0.15)^{0}(0.85)^{18-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{18}{1})(0.15)^{1}(0.85)^{18-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.85)^{18}+16(0.15)(0.85)^{17}] \\\\  & =1-0.284\\,053 \\\\  & =0.776\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186625595 1071 "A water tank leaked some water. After $55%$ of the original water leaked out, $360$ liters remained. How many liters leaked out?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186625595 1071 "A water tank leaked some water. After $55\\%$ of the original water leaked out, $360$ liters remained. How many liters leaked out?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186625595 1074 "Decreasing a number by $55%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%-55%=45%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n45%=\\frac{45}{100}=0.45.\n$$\n\nLet the original liters of water be $x.$ So, after a $55%$ decrease, the new value is \n\n$$\n0.45x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $360$ liters remained. So, we have the equation\n\n$$\n0.45x=360.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.45x & =360 \\\\ x & =\\frac{360}{0.45} \\\\ x & =800\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the original amount was $800$ liters, we find the amount removed by taking $55%$ of $800:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{55}{100}\\cdot 800=440.\n$$\n\nSo, $440$ liters leaked out." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186625595 1074 "Decreasing a number by $55\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%-55\\%=45\\%.\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n45\\%=\\frac{45}{100}=0.45.\n$$\n\nLet the original liters of water be $x.$ So, after a $55\\%$ decrease, the new value is 