ng probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.25)^{x-1}(0.25) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{x-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of hitting the bullseye in one of the first $3$ shoots is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{1-1}(0.25)+(0.75)^{2-1}(0.25)+(0.75)^{3-1}(0.25) \\\\  & =(0.25)+(0.75)(0.25)+(0.75)^{2}(0.25) \\\\  & =[1+0.75+(0.75)^{2}](0.25) \\\\  & ≈0.578\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186628432 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of shoots before hitting the bullseye. Since the chance of hitting the bullseye is $25\\%,$ the number of shoots required to hit the bullseye can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=25\\%=\\frac{25}{100}=0.25.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.25).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.25)^{x-1}(0.25) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{x-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of hitting the bullseye in one of the first $3$ shoots is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{1-1}(0.25)+(0.75)^{2-1}(0.25)+(0.75)^{3-1}(0.25) \\\\  & =(0.25)+(0.75)(0.25)+(0.75)^{2}(0.25) \\\\  & =[1+0.75+(0.75)^{2}](0.25) \\\\  & ≈0.578\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186628449 1071 "A book is being randomly chosen from a backpack and put back in until getting a math book. $35%$ of the books in the backpack are math books. What is the probability that a math book is chosen in one of the first $3$ tries?  Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186628449 1071 "A book is being randomly chosen from a backpack and put back in until getting a math book. $35\\%$ of the books in the backpack are math books. What is the probability that a math book is chosen in one of the first $3$ tries?  Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186628449 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of random choices made before getting a math book. Since the chance of getting a math book is $35%,$ the number of choices required to get a math book can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=35%=\\frac{35}{100}=0.35.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.35).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.35)^{x-1}(0.35) \\\\  & =(0.65)^{x-1}(0.35)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of choosing a math book in one of the first $3$ tries is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.65)^{1-1}(0.35)+(0.65)^{2-1}(0.35)+(0.65)^{3-1}(0.35) \\\\  & =(0.35)+(0.65)(0.35)+(0.65)^{2}(0.35) \\\\  & =[1+0.65+(0.65)^{2}](0.35) \\\\  & ≈0.725\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186628449 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of random choices made before getting a math book. Since the chance of getting a math book is $35\\%,$ the number of choices required to get a math book can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=35\\%=\\frac{35}{100}=0.35.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.35).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.35)^{x-1}(0.35) \\\\  & =(0.65)^{x-1}(0.35)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of choosing a math book in one of the first $3$ tries is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.65)^{1-1}(0.35)+(0.65)^{2-1}(0.35)+(0.65)^{3-1}(0.35) \\\\  & =(0.35)+(0.65)(0.35)+(0.65)^{2}(0.35) \\\\  & =[1+0.65+(0.65)^{2}](0.35) \\\\  & ≈0.725\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186628499 1071 "Billy ran some scripts on his computer. He started by running $300$ scripts, and the number of scripts remaining decreased by $2%$ after every minute. To the nearest integer, how many scripts were running after $4$ minutes?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186628499 1071 "Billy ran some scripts on his computer. He started by running $300$ scripts, and the number of scripts remaining decreased by $2\\%$ after every minute. To the nearest integer, how many scripts were running after $4$ minutes?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186628499 1074 "The number $2%$ (expressed as a decimal) is equal to $0.02.$ Therefore, a decrease of $2%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of:\n\n$$\n1-0.02=0.98\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ minute, $300\\cdot (0.98)$ scripts were running.\n\n- After $2$ minutes,  $300\\cdot (0.98)^{2}$ scripts were running.\n\n- After $3$ minutes, $300\\cdot (0.98)^{3}$ scripts were running.\n\n- After $4$ minutes, $300\\cdot (0.98)^{4}$ scripts were running.\n\nEvaluating the last expression gives\n\n$$\n300\\cdot (0.98)^{4}≈277.\n$$\n\nSo, there were $277$ scripts running after $4$ minutes." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186628499 1074 "The number $2\\%$ (expressed as a decimal) is equal to $0.02.$ Therefore, a decrease of $2\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of:\n\n$$\n1-0.02=0.98\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ minute, $300\\cdot (0.98)$ scripts were running.\n\n- After $2$ minutes,  $300\\cdot (0.98)^{2}$ scripts were running.\n\n- After $3$ minutes, $300\\cdot (0.98)^{3}$ scripts were running.\n\n- After $4$ minutes, $300\\cdot (0.98)^{4}$ scripts were running.\n\nEvaluating the last expression gives\n\n$$\n300\\cdot (0.98)^{4}≈277.\n$$\n\nSo, there were $277$ scripts running after $4$ minutes." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186629255 1071 "A smartphone manufacturer tests its screens for tiny scratches before assembly. On average, $18%$ of the screens are found to be scratched. Each screen is tested independently of the others. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that the first scratched screen is found \non the $4$th or later tested screen is{{field-1}}." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186629255 1071 "A smartphone manufacturer tests its screens for tiny scratches before assembly. On average, $18\\%$ of the screens are found to be scratched. Each screen is tested independently of the others. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that the first scratched screen is found \non the $4$th or later tested screen is{{field-1}}." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186629255 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of screens tested until the first scratched screen is found. Since the chance that any single screen is scratched is $18%,$ the number of screens tested until the first scratched screen is found can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=18%=\\frac{18}{100}=0.18.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.18).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.18)^{x-1}(0.18) \\\\  & =(0.82)^{x-1}(0.18)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 4),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 4)=1-P(X<4)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.82)^{1-1}(0.18)+(0.82)^{2-1}(0.18)+(0.82)^{3-1}(0.18) \\\\  & =[1+(0.82)+(0.82)^{2}](0.18) \\\\  & ≈0.449,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability that the first scratched screen is found on the $4$th or later tested screen is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 4) & =1-P(X<4) \\\\  & ≈1-0.449 \\\\  & ≈0.551,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186629255 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of screens tested until the first scratched screen is found. Since the chance that any single screen is scratched is $18\\%,$ the number of screens tested until the first scratched screen is found can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=18\\%=\\frac{18}{100}=0.18.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.18).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.18)^{x-1}(0.18) \\\\  & =(0.82)^{x-1}(0.18)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 4),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 4)=1-P(X<4)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.82)^{1-1}(0.18)+(0.82)^{2-1}(0.18)+(0.82)^{3-1}(0.18) \\\\  & =[1+(0.82)+(0.82)^{2}](0.18) \\\\  & ≈0.449,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability that the first scratched screen is found on the $4$th or later tested screen is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 4) & =1-P(X<4) \\\\  & ≈1-0.449 \\\\  & ≈0.551,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186629274 1071 "The percentage $p$ at which a pool is filled is directly proportional to the filling time $t,$ measured in hours ($h$). When the filling time is $t=8\\,h,$ the percentage fill is $p=60%.$ Find the filling time when the percentage is $p=100%.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186629274 1071 "The percentage $p$ at which a pool is filled is directly proportional to the filling time $t,$ measured in hours ($h$). When the filling time is $t=8\\,h,$ the percentage fill is $p=60\\%.$ Find the filling time when the percentage is $p=100\\%.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186629274 1074 "The percentage fill $p$ is directly proportional to the time $t,$ so we have\n\n$$\np=kt.\n$$\n\nWe are told that when the time is $t=8\\,h,$ the percentage fill is $p=60%.$  We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =k\\cdot (8) \\\\ 60 & =8k \\\\ \\frac{60}{8} & =k \\\\ 7.5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\np=7.5t.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the time when the percentage is $p=100%,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{100}{7.5}=13+\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time when $p=100%$  is $t=13\\,\\text{h}\\,20\\,\\text{min}.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186629274 1074 "The percentage fill $p$ is directly proportional to the time $t,$ so we have\n\n$$\np=kt.\n$$\n\nWe are told that when the time is $t=8\\,h,$ the percentage fill is $p=60\\%.$  We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =k\\cdot (8) \\\\ 60 & =8k \\\\ \\frac{60}{8} & =k \\\\ 7.5 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\np=7.5t.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the time when the percentage is $p=100\\%,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{100}{7.5}=13+\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time when $p=100\\%$  is $t=13\\,\\text{h}\\,20\\,\\text{min}.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186629342 1071 "David is randomly picking a crayon from a box and putting it back in until he gets a green crayon. Approximately, $15%$ of the crayons in the box are green. What is the probability that the $5$th crayon is the first green crayon picked? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13