eces of candy that he shares with his friends. The number of candy pieces in Roy's bag decreases by $37.5\\%$ every day. How many days will it take until there are only $50$ pieces of candy left in Roy's bag?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186632674 1074 "The number of pieces of candy $y(t)$ decays exponentially with a decay rate of $37.5%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=128$ is the initial number of candy.\n\n- $r=0.375$ is the decay rate ($37.5%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in days.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=128\\cdot (1-0.375)^{t}=128\\cdot {(0.625)}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSince $0.625=\\frac{5}{8},$ this is equivalent to\n\n$$\ny(t)=128\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe want to know how many days it takes for the number of pieces of candy to decrease to $50$, so we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n128\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{t}=50.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}128\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{t} & =50 \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{t} & =\\frac{50}{128} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{t} & =\\frac{25}{64} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{t} & =\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{8}^{2}} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{t} & ={\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, it takes $2$ days for the number of candy pieces to decrease to $50.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186632674 1074 "The number of pieces of candy $y(t)$ decays exponentially with a decay rate of $37.5\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=128$ is the initial number of candy.\n\n- $r=0.375$ is the decay rate ($37.5\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in days.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=128\\cdot (1-0.375)^{t}=128\\cdot {(0.625)}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSince $0.625=\\frac{5}{8},$ this is equivalent to\n\n$$\ny(t)=128\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe want to know how many days it takes for the number of pieces of candy to decrease to $50$, so we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n128\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{t}=50.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}128\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{t} & =50 \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{t} & =\\frac{50}{128} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{t} & =\\frac{25}{64} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{t} & =\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{8}^{2}} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{t} & ={\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, it takes $2$ days for the number of candy pieces to decrease to $50.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186632687 1071 "A vacation house was first valued at $\\$100\\,000.$ Every year its value decreases by $10%.$ How many years will pass until the house value is $\\$72\\,900?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186632687 1071 "A vacation house was first valued at $\\$100\\,000.$ Every year its value decreases by $10\\%.$ How many years will pass until the house value is $\\$72\\,900?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186632687 1074 "The vacation house's value $v(t)$ decays exponentially with a decay rate of $10%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nv(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=100\\,000$ is the initial value of the house, in dollars.\n\n- $r=0.1$ is the decay rate ($10%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $v$ is\n\n$$\nv(t)=100\\,000(1-0.1)^{t}=100\\,000(0.9)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSince $0.9=\\frac{9}{10},$ this is equivalent to\n\n$$\nv(t)=100\\,000{\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe want to know how many years it takes for the house's value to decrease to $\\$72\\,900,$ so we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n100\\,000{\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{t}=72\\,900.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100\\,000{\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{t} & =72\\,900 \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{t} & =\\frac{72\\,900}{100\\,000} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{t} & =\\frac{729}{1000} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{t} & =\\frac{{9}^{3}}{{10}^{3}} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{t} & ={\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{3} \\\\ t & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, it takes $3$ years for the vacation house value to decrease to $\\$72\\,900.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186632687 1074 "The vacation house's value $v(t)$ decays exponentially with a decay rate of $10\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nv(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=100\\,000$ is the initial value of the house, in dollars.\n\n- $r=0.1$ is the decay rate ($10\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $v$ is\n\n$$\nv(t)=100\\,000(1-0.1)^{t}=100\\,000(0.9)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSince $0.9=\\frac{9}{10},$ this is equivalent to\n\n$$\nv(t)=100\\,000{\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe want to know how many years it takes for the house's value to decrease to $\\$72\\,900,$ so we need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n100\\,000{\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{t}=72\\,900.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100\\,000{\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{t} & =72\\,900 \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{t} & =\\frac{72\\,900}{100\\,000} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{t} & =\\frac{729}{1000} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{t} & =\\frac{{9}^{3}}{{10}^{3}} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{t} & ={\\left(\\frac{9}{10}\\right)}^{3} \\\\ t & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, it takes $3$ years for the vacation house value to decrease to $\\$72\\,900.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186632688 1071 "If\n    $$\n\\displ